You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 3 — STUDENT ROSTER MANAGEMENT & IMPORT

The goal of this phase is to implement student roster management for an
exam.

This phase establishes the student data required for later:

- Room assignment
- Seat assignment
- Seating maps
- Signature lists
- Personalized exam generation
- Crowdmark-related workflows

IMPORTANT:

Keep the implementation simple, secure, deterministic, and professional.

Do NOT implement seating assignment yet.

Do NOT implement PDF generation yet.

Do NOT implement Crowdmark integration yet.

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md

Also inspect the existing implementation from:

- Phase 0
- Phase 1
- Phase 2

Follow existing project patterns.

Do not introduce a new architecture.

Before modifying code, provide a concise implementation plan.

==================================================
2. PHASE 3 SCOPE
==================================================

Implement:

1. Student entity
2. Exam roster
3. Student roster import
4. CSV import
5. Roster validation
6. Duplicate detection
7. Roster preview
8. Import confirmation
9. Roster viewing
10. Roster removal where appropriate
11. Basic student search
12. Roster statistics
13. Authorization
14. Audit events
15. Database migrations
16. Backend APIs
17. Frontend UI
18. Automated tests

Do NOT implement:

- Room management
- Seat management
- Seating assignment
- Seating algorithms
- PDF generation
- Personalized exam generation
- Crowdmark API integration
- QR code processing
- Signature list generation
- Seating map generation

==================================================
3. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLE
==================================================

ExamFlow is an examination administration system.

Student data is sensitive.

Therefore:

- Minimize stored data.
- Never expose student information unnecessarily.
- Never log full student records.
- Never send student data to external AI services.
- Never commit real student data to Git.
- Use synthetic data in development and tests.

==================================================
4. STUDENT MODEL
==================================================

Create a Student entity.

Minimum expected information:

id
student_number
first_name
last_name
display_name
created_at
updated_at

Follow doc/prd.md and doc/DATABASE.md if they define different fields.

Do not add unnecessary fields such as:

- address
- phone
- date of birth
- personal email
- nationality
- program
- GPA

unless explicitly required by the product requirements.

ExamFlow is not a student information system.

==================================================
5. STUDENT IDENTIFIER
==================================================

Student number is the primary institutional identifier used by ExamFlow.

It must be:

- required
- normalized
- unique

Do not use student name as the unique identifier.

Names can be duplicated.

Do not assume a specific student-number format unless defined by requirements.

Avoid overly strict formatting rules.

==================================================
6. STUDENT NAME
==================================================

Store names in a way that supports reasonable display and searching.

At minimum:

first_name
last_name

A display_name may be generated or stored according to the architecture.

Do not create complicated international-name parsing logic.

Do not assume every student's name follows the same cultural naming pattern.

==================================================
7. STUDENT PRIVACY
==================================================

Student data must be treated as sensitive institutional information.

Do NOT:

- log student names unnecessarily
- log student numbers
- include student information in error messages unnecessarily
- put student information into URLs unnecessarily
- expose the entire roster to unauthorized users
- include real students in seed data
- send student data to external services

Use synthetic data for tests.

==================================================
8. EXAM ROSTER RELATIONSHIP
==================================================

An exam must have a roster.

Do not assume that a Student belongs to only one exam.

The same student may take:

- multiple exams
- multiple exams in different terms
- different exams in the same course

Therefore:

Student

and

Exam

should be modeled as a relationship.

Do not duplicate complete student records for every exam.

==================================================
9. EXAM REGISTRATION / ROSTER ENTITY
==================================================

Create an appropriate relationship entity between Student and Exam.

Suggested concept:

ExamStudent

or:

ExamEnrollment

or:

ExamRosterEntry

Follow the project's naming conventions.

This entity may contain:

id
exam_id
student_id
created_at
updated_at

Additional fields should only be added if required.

Do not add room or seat fields yet.

==================================================
10. UNIQUE ROSTER MEMBERSHIP
==================================================

A student should not appear twice in the same exam roster.

The database must enforce:

unique(exam_id, student_id)

Do not rely only on frontend validation.

==================================================
11. ROSTER IMPORT SOURCE
==================================================

Phase 3 should support CSV roster import.

Do not implement direct integration with:

- Registrar systems
- LMS
- SIS
- PeopleSoft
- Workday
- external APIs

unless explicitly required.

File-based CSV import keeps Phase 3 simple and makes it useful immediately.

==================================================
12. CSV FORMAT
==================================================

Support a clearly documented CSV format.

For example:

student_number,first_name,last_name

100000001,Alice,Student
100000002,Bob,Student

Do not require unnecessary columns.

Document the exact expected CSV format.

==================================================
13. CSV HEADER

The importer should support a header row.

Expected fields should be clearly defined.

Do not silently guess arbitrary column meanings.

If reasonable, support case-insensitive header matching.

For example:

Student Number
student_number
STUDENT_NUMBER

may be treated equivalently if the implementation remains simple.

Document the behavior.

==================================================
14. CSV ENCODING

Support UTF-8 CSV.

Handle UTF-8 BOM if reasonably possible.

Do not attempt to support every possible legacy encoding in Phase 3.

If an unsupported encoding is detected, return a useful error.

==================================================
15. CSV DELIMITER

Use comma as the default delimiter.

Do not automatically guess dozens of delimiter types unless there is a
clear requirement.

If delimiter configuration is needed, keep it explicit.

==================================================
16. CSV VALIDATION

Before importing, validate the entire file.

Validate:

- required columns
- missing student number
- duplicate student numbers within the file
- invalid empty rows
- excessive field lengths
- malformed CSV
- invalid data

Do not partially import a file if validation fails.

Preferred workflow:

Upload
  ↓
Parse
  ↓
Validate
  ↓
Preview
  ↓
Confirm
  ↓
Import
